  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/6e7ef0d4665710a31c1c601048498f20ad6b3099769c1d140320074805964ec7.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Caltech and car $A$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Caltech and car $B$,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.8\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.6\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.8}^{2}+{0.6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.8)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.6)(-60) & =2(1)(20) \\\\ 1.6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40+72 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{112}{1.6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car $A$ is $70\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$",
